description:
Build `Ring<T>`, a double-ended queue stored in a ring buffer that grows when full:

```cpp
template <class T>
class Ring
{
public:
    void push_back(T value);
    void push_front(T value);
    T pop_back();               // std::out_of_range if empty
    T pop_front();              // std::out_of_range if empty
    T& front();  T& back();     // std::out_of_range if empty
    T& operator[](std::size_t i);   // i counts from the front
    std::size_t size() const;
    std::size_t capacity() const;
};
```

- Keep the elements in a `std::vector<T>` of slots, with the index of the front element (`head_`) and the number of elements (`size_`). Element `i` lives in slot `(head_ + i) % capacity`.
- `push_front` steps `head_` back by one, **wrapping** from 0 to `capacity - 1`.
- When a push finds the ring full, grow to capacity 4 (if it was 0) or double it, copying (moving) the elements across **in queue order** so that the front ends up in slot 0.
- Every operation is O(1), amortised for pushes. `T` may be assumed to be default-constructible and movable.

